    title: "The Delta-Sigma ADC — 20 Bits From a 1-Bit Comparator",
    tagline: "A first-principles, application-complete deep dive — you cannot build a 20-bit converter by trimming 20-bit-accurate components (nobody matches a million levels to <1 ppm), so delta-sigma stops trying. It uses the crudest quantizer there is — a 1-bit comparator (two levels → nothing to mismatch → perfectly LINEAR) — runs it far faster than Nyquist (oversampling), and wraps it in a feedback loop with an integrator that high-pass-filters its OWN quantization noise out of the signal band (noise shaping). Two stacked moves: oversampling spreads the fixed quantization-noise power thin (OSR = f_s/2f_B, ~0.5 bit per octave), and noise shaping of order L bends it out of band (~(6L+3) dB/octave → 1.5 bits/octave 1st-order, 2.5 bits/octave 2nd-order), so a 1-bit comparator clocked fast inside a 2nd-order loop gives 16–20+ bits. The modulator (delta: subtract the fed-back bit; sigma: integrate; compare) emits a 1-bit stream whose DENSITY of ones encodes the input — the value is the average — and a digital decimation filter averages + kills the shaped HF noise + downsamples. Then the practitioner's half — resolution and speed are the same knob (bandwidth traded for bits), troubleshooting (noisy at high data rate = OSR dropped; blended channels after a mux switch = sinc filter settling memory; a tone = idle tones on DC input; hum = pick a rate with 50/60 Hz notches), how it moves difficulty from analog matching into cheap digital logic and relaxes anti-aliasing to a single RC, and what to spec (data rate/bandwidth, ENOB at that rate, order/OSR, integrated PGA + reference, filter options, latency); plus field notes (at 24 bits the reference/PGA/board — not the modulator — are your noise floor; '24-bit' is marketing until you say at what rate). Build it: a 24-bit load-cell / weigh-scale front end end-to-end (~10 mV full-scale, 0–5 Hz, PGA 128×, 10 SPS for max bits + free 50/60 Hz notches, ~19–21 noise-free bits, ratiometric to cancel excitation drift), the plug-in formulas (OSR, (6L+3) dB/oct, ENOB ≈ (SNR−1.76)/6.02), a bring-up recipe (measure the shorted-input noise floor first), the forks (ΔΣ vs SAR vs pipeline, data rate, sinc vs sharp filter), and the honest last mile (noise floor is a layout/reference problem; know your filter settling; calibrate to a traceable reference). With live sims of the 1-bit modulator's ones-density tracking the input, and noise shaping pushing quantization noise out of band as OSR and order rise. Deep Dive #09.",
