    title: "The DC-Motor Speed–Torque Line — Why a Motor Is a Torque Source in Disguise",
    tagline: "A first-principles, application-complete deep dive — ask how fast a motor spins and how hard it pulls and the honest answer is a single straight line. A brushed DC motor obeys just two linear laws: torque follows current (T = k_t·I) and the spinning motor fights back with a back-EMF proportional to speed (V_emf = k_e·ω, and in SI k_e = k_t). So the current is whatever is left after the back-EMF opposes the supply: I = (V − k_e·ω)/R — the motor self-regulates its current through its own speed. Combine them and speed is a straight line in torque: ω = V/k_e − (R/k_t·k_e)·T, running from NO-LOAD SPEED ω₀ = V/k_e (zero torque) to STALL TORQUE T_s = k_t·V/R (zero speed), slope set by R. A motor is really a torque (current) source wearing a speed disguise; raise the voltage and the whole line slides up (that's PWM speed control, Deep Dive #07). Limit checks: no-load = fast/cool/lazy; stall = max current and 100% heat (cooks in seconds, inrush 5–10× rated); max output power at mid-line; max efficiency up near no-load; the real limit is a thermal BOX inside the line (continuous vs peak), because heat = (T/k_t)²·R grows with torque². Then the practitioner's half — sizing (load torque/speed → voltage → operating point in the continuous box → current/inrush → gearing), troubleshooting (sags under load = steep line/high R; hot = near stall/over continuous; won't start = stall torque below breakaway; trips on startup = inrush = V/R; top speed low = need volts not 'power'), how voltage/R/winding/gearing/thermal are all positions and slopes on the one line, and what to spec (continuous torque/speed, stall torque & current, k_t/k_e, R and the motor constant k_m = k_t/√R, thermal region); plus field notes (command current = command torque; stall is a thermal event not an operating point; compare motors by k_m not watts; gearing is the cheapest torque; the line sags with a hot winding; 'enough stall torque' is a trap). Build it: sizing a 12 V robot gearmotor end-to-end (~5 kg at 1 m/s on 100 mm wheels → 0.5 N·m / ~190 rpm at the wheel, 6000 rpm no-load motor, N≈20–30:1, inrush 12V/1.5Ω = 8 A, size the driver for stall not running current), the plug-in formulas, a bring-up recipe (measure k_e from no-load speed, R with a meter — now you own its line), the forks (direct vs geared, winding choice, volts vs ratio, brushed vs BLDC), and the honest last mile (measure real + hot-winding constants, worst-case load, RMS-torque thermal math). With live sims of the speed–torque line sliding with voltage and the operating point riding it, and the motor line meeting a load curve with the continuous-duty box shaded. Deep Dive #10.",
