    id: "dd-bolted",
    file: "whathappens-dd-bolted.html",
    emoji: "🔩",
    title: "Bolted-Joint Preload — Why a Bolt Is a Spring You Pre-Stretch, Not a Hook",
    tagline: "A first-principles, application-complete deep dive — bolts snap in service far below their rated strength, vibrate loose, and let joints leak while being 'strong enough' for the load, because the naive model (the bolt is a hook you hang the load on) is simply wrong. The reframe: a tightened bolt is a stiff SPRING you pre-stretch, and that stretch clamps the members with a force — the PRELOAD F_i — before any external load appears. The members are springs too (usually stiffer), compressed by the same F_i, so a joint is two opposing springs in equilibrium. When an external load P is applied it does NOT go into the bolt — it SPLITS by the stiffness ratio: the bolt force rises by only C·P and the member clamp drops by (1−C)·P, where the load factor C = k_b/(k_b+k_m). Because metal members are far stiffer than the slender bolt, C ≈ 0.1–0.3, so a 10 kN external pull adds only 1–3 kN to the bolt — the preloaded bolt barely feels the fluctuating load, which is exactly what kills fatigue and loosening. Counterintuitively, tightening a bolt MORE makes it last LONGER (higher preload keeps the joint closed so C does its job), up to ~75% of proof load; the joint separates and the protection vanishes only when P > F_i/(1−C). Then the practitioner's half — designing from the required clamp (not the bolt rating), troubleshooting (fatigue fracture = preload too low or C too high / soft gasket; backing off = insufficient preload vs vibration; leak/slip = preload decayed to embedment/creep; snapped on install = over-torqued past yield), how preload and C set fatigue life, loosening resistance, slip resistance and separation margin, and what to spec (grade, target preload %, tightening method & nut factor, grip length/washers, gasket, locking, re-torque); plus field notes (tighten more to last longer; you control torque but want tension and the nut factor scatters ±30%; short fat bolts are fatigue-prone — go longer/slenderer to drop C; gaskets are fatigue killers; lube lowers K so re-target torque; don't reuse torque-to-yield bolts). Build it: an M10 class-8.8 bracket under a 0–10 kN fluctuating pull, end-to-end (proof 580 MPa, A_t 58 mm², F_p 33.6 kN → F_i 25 kN at 75% proof, C=0.2 → F_bolt only 27 kN / 466 MPa, fatigue amplitude σ_a ≈ 17 MPa, separation at 31 kN, torque 50 N·m at K=0.2 ±30%), the plug-in formulas, a bring-up recipe (set the clamp need first, then grade, then check C for fatigue, re-torque after settling), the forks (torque vs angle vs stretch/ultrasonic, grade 8.8/10.9/12.9, tune C with grip length, gasket & locking), and the honest last mile (the nut factor drifts — measure it or use angle/stretch; member stiffness via VDI 2230/FEA; plan for 5–10% embedment loss; verify critical joints ultrasonically). With live sims of the joint diagram splitting an external load between bolt and clamp, and the bolt riding a thin fatigue band above a high preload until it separates. Deep Dive #13.",
