    id: "dd-hohmann",
    file: "whathappens-dd-hohmann.html",
    emoji: "🛰️",
    title: "The Hohmann Transfer — Why You Speed Up to Reach a Slower, Higher Orbit",
    tagline: "A first-principles, application-complete deep dive — you're in one circular orbit and want a higher one, and every instinct from flying (point where you want to go and thrust) is exactly wrong in orbit. Two facts drive everything: an orbit's size is set by its ENERGY (burn prograde → it grows), and circular speed FALLS with altitude (v = √(μ/r), so high orbits are slow orbits). So the cheapest route is: burn prograde at your altitude to raise the opposite side of the orbit into an ellipse that reaches the target, coast the free half-ellipse up to apogee, then burn prograde AGAIN to circularize — two prograde burns, one coasting ellipse. Join the two circles with a transfer ellipse tangent to both (a = (r₁+r₂)/2); the one equation that gives every speed is vis-viva v = √(μ(2/r − 1/a)); Δv₁ = v_p − v₁ at perigee, Δv₂ = v₂ − v_a at apogee, and the trip takes t = π√(a³/μ). This is the minimum-Δv two-impulse transfer between circular orbits (up to a ratio of ~11.9, beyond which a bi-elliptic wins). The punchline is a paradox: you fire the engine to ADD speed at both burns, yet you end up SLOWER than you started — because the burns pour velocity into altitude (energy up, speed down). Then the practitioner's half — planning a maneuver (r₁, r₂ → a → four speeds → two Δv's → Δv budget → rocket equation → propellant; plus transfer time and phase-angle/launch window), troubleshooting (right altitude but target absent = phasing; budget blows up on an inclined target = unbudgeted plane change — do it at slow apogee; 'costs more than Hohmann' = non-tangential burns or huge ratio; didn't circularize = skipped Δv₂; low-thrust stage = must spiral), how the numbers ripple into propellant mass, mission duration, windows and propulsion choice, and what to spec (the Δv's, transfer time, phase angle, plane-change Δv, thrust/Isp, margins, bi-elliptic check); plus field notes (burn prograde to go up and accept you'll be slower; both burns must be tangential; do plane changes where you're slow; the free coast isn't free in time; windows not whims; electric propulsion spirals, it doesn't Hohmann; vis-viva is the one equation to carry; the ideal Δv is a floor — add 5–15%). Build it: LEO → GEO end-to-end (μ=398600, r₁=6778 km, r₂=42164 km, a=24471 km → v₁ 7.67, v₂ 3.07, v_p 10.07, v_a 1.62, Δv₁ 2.40, Δv₂ 1.45, total ≈3.85 km/s, t ≈5.3 h), the plug-in formulas, a planning recipe, the forks (Hohmann vs bi-elliptic, chemical-impulsive vs electric-spiral, where to spend the plane change, direct vs parking orbit), and the honest last mile (finite-burn losses, phasing with real ephemerides, GMAT/STK/poliastro/pykep). With live sims of the transfer geometry + both burns, and the vis-viva speed ladder that makes the paradox visible. Deep Dive #14.",
