    id: "dd-transmission",
    file: "whathappens-dd-transmission.html",
    emoji: "〽️",
    title: "Transmission-Line Reflections — When a Wire Stops Being a Wire",
    tagline: "A first-principles, application-complete deep dive — the same board that worked at 1 MHz rings, overshoots and double-clocks at 100 MHz with nothing changed but the edge speed. The hidden assumption that breaks is that a wire is instantaneous: a voltage step travels at a FINITE speed (~15 cm/ns in a PCB, ~half the speed of light), so while the wave is in flight different points of the wire are at different voltages at the same time — the wire is a TRANSMISSION LINE, not a lumped node. A traveling wave carries a fixed V/I = Z₀ = √(L/C) (the characteristic impedance, ~50 Ω SE / 100 Ω diff), so the line 'looks like' a resistor Z₀ to the driver while the wave travels. When the wave hits a load Z_L it can't satisfy both V/I = Z₀ and V/I = Z_L, so a REFLECTED wave is launched back by Γ = (Z_L − Z₀)/(Z_L + Z₀): +1 at an open (voltage DOUBLES), −1 at a short, and 0 when matched (Z_L = Z₀ — the whole cure). Reflections re-reflect off the source and ring, stepped by the round-trip time t_rt = 2ℓ/v. The criterion for when a wire becomes a line: it's ELECTRICALLY LONG (and must be terminated) when the round-trip delay exceeds the RISE TIME — it's the rise time, not the clock frequency, that decides. Kill reflections by matching an end: series/source termination (R_s = Z₀ − R_driver, launches a half-step that doubles at the open far end — clean at the end, no DC power) or parallel/end termination (= Z₀ at the load — clean everywhere, burns DC power). Then the practitioner's half — the design flow (rise time → round-trip vs rise time → controlled Z₀ → termination → minimize stubs/vias → simulate + scope/TDR), troubleshooting (ringing/overshoot = unterminated; staircase = reflections or series mid-line; double-clocking = ring through threshold; 'works slow fails fast' = rise time crossed the threshold; mid-path reflections = a connector/via/stub; EMI = the ringing radiating), how Z₀ and rise time drive the stack-up, routing and power budget, and what to spec (Z₀, rise/fall time, termination type, length vs round-trip, discontinuities, loss/crosstalk, return-path continuity); plus field notes (reason from the EDGE; an open far end is the worst and the default; series termination is clean only at the far end; the return current flows right under the trace — never cross a plane gap; stubs are stealth reflectors; a TDR is the truth machine; 50 Ω isn't sacred — consistency is). Build it: a 100 MHz clock on a 20 cm trace with a 0.5 ns edge, end-to-end (one-way 1.33 ns, t_rt ≈ 2.67 ns ≫ 0.5 ns → electrically long → series-terminate with R_s = 50 − 20 = 30 Ω at the driver), the plug-in formulas, a bring-up recipe, the forks (series vs parallel, terminate vs slow the edge, single-ended vs differential, controlled-impedance or not), and the honest last mile (real stack-up Z₀ from a field solver, IBIS/SPICE SI simulation, scope + TDR). With live sims of a step bouncing off the load and source (ringing that vanishes at Z_L = Z₀) and the electrically-long threshold flipping between a lumped wire and a ringing line. Deep Dive #15.",
